<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guanabara Bay</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Baía de Guanabara</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Guanabara Bay is a large bay on the south-eastern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, on whose shores stand the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a> and Niterói. In 1555 French settlers under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolas-durand-de-villegaignon">Nicolas Durand de Villegaignon</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> Antarctique on an island in the bay. The Portuguese expelled them in 1560–1567 and founded Rio de Janeiro, which grew into the colonial capital of Brazil.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guanabara-bay.jpg" alt="Guanabara Bay" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rio_deJaneiro_LE2002059_lrg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Guanabara Bay is an oceanic bay in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, in the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a>. Rio de Janeiro and Duque de Caxias lie on its western shore, and Niterói and São Gonçalo on its eastern shore. It is the second largest bay in Brazil by area, after All Saints' Bay, covering 384 square kilometres with a perimeter of 143 kilometres. It is 31 kilometres long and 28 kilometres wide at most. Its mouth, 1.5 kilometres wide, is flanked by the Pico do Papagaio on the eastern tip and the Pão de Açúcar, or Sugar Loaf, on the western tip.</p><p>The name comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupi">Tupi</a> language, goanã-pará, from gwa, bay, nã, similar to, and ba'ra, sea. Other suggested meanings include hidden water, lagoon of the sea and bosom of the sea. More than 130 islands dot the bay.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Tamoio and Tupiniquim people lived on the shores of the bay when the Portuguese explorers Gaspar de Lemos and Gonçalo Coelho first reached it on 1 January 1502, and they named it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a>. It is widely believed that they mistook the bay for a river mouth, but they probably knew it was a bay and called it a river because the term was then applied to other bodies of water, such as bays and rias.</p><p>No significant European settlement followed until 1555, when French colonists and soldiers under the Huguenot admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolas-durand-de-villegaignon">Nicolas Durand de Villegaignon</a> invaded to found France Antarctique. They stayed briefly on Lajes Island and then moved to Serigipe Island, near the shore, where they built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-coligny">Fort Coligny</a>. After Portuguese military expeditions expelled them, the colonial government built fortifications at several points on the bay, among them the forts of Santa Cruz, São João, Lajes and Villegaignon, which formed a crossfire of heavy naval guns and made the bay almost impregnable to attack from the sea. Other islands were later fitted by the Navy for storehouses, hospitals, drydocks, oil <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a> and the National Naval Academy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Rio-Niterói Bridge, 13.29 kilometres long, crosses the bay, and ferries and ships use it heavily. The Port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a> and the city's two airports, Galeão on Governador Island and Santos Dumont on reclaimed land, stand on its shores, and the main campus of the Federal University of Rio de Janeiro is on the artificial Fundão Island. The Guapimirim Environmental Protection Area protects part of it, chiefly in the municipality of Guapimirim.</p><p>Urbanisation, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deforestation">deforestation</a> and pollution have damaged its mangroves, and an oil leak from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrobras">Petrobras</a> pipeline in 2000 released 1,300,000 litres into the bay. It was once a whaling ground, and a population of botos, river dolphins, still lives there.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Guanabara Bay” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sailing along the Brazilian coast, a Portuguese expedition entered <b>Guanabara Bay</b> and, taking it for a river mouth, called it the 'River of January'.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-1">1 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After Portuguese forces drove the French of France Antarctique from <b>Guanabara Bay</b>, the crown established the Captaincy of Rio de Janeiro in 1567 as a royal rather than hereditary captaincy.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-of-rio-de-janeiro">Captaincy of Rio de Janeiro</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1555 the French admiral Nicolas Durand de Villegaignon, backed by Admiral Gaspard de Coligny, sailed to <b>Guanabara Bay</b> in Brazil with some 600 settlers.</blockquote><a href="/state/france-antarctique">France Antarctique</a></li></ul></section>
